WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.492
A permitted destination does not make
every request safe.

2
00:00:03.572 --> 00:00:07.968
Define an operation-specific method and
body policy instead of accepting arbitrary

3
00:00:08.048 --> 00:00:09.086
verbs and payloads.

4
00:00:09.166 --> 00:00:12.280
Set connection and response deadlines, cap
redirect count,

5
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.642
stream into a byte limit,

6
00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:17.752
and bound decompression so a small
compressed response cannot expand without

7
00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:18.321
control.

8
00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:22.736
Enforce request-body limits and
content-type expectations where they support the

9
00:00:22.816 --> 00:00:23.304
feature.

10
00:00:23.384 --> 00:00:26.499
These measures reduce resource abuse and
reliability risk;

11
00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:29.570
they are not substitutes for destination
authorization.

12
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.276
Headers and credentials need their own
allowlist.

13
00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:36.264
A generic outbound helper should not copy
inbound cookies, Authorization,

14
00:00:36.344 --> 00:00:38.664
forwarding headers, or internal API
tokens.

15
00:00:38.744 --> 00:00:43.018
Credentials for a known provider should
come from the server-managed integration

16
00:00:43.098 --> 00:00:47.067
record and be attached only after
destination and operation authorization.

17
00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.711
Custom certificate trust, client
certificates,

18
00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:52.783
and service identity are similarly scoped
capabilities.

19
00:00:52.863 --> 00:00:55.000
Log enough to diagnose policy decisions,

20
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.767
but redact credentials and sensitive query
values.

21
00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:00.106
Proxies change the effective network path.

22
00:01:00.186 --> 00:01:03.667
Environment-driven proxy settings, an
explicit corporate proxy,

23
00:01:03.747 --> 00:01:08.021
or a service mesh may resolve names and
establish connections somewhere other than

24
00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.444
the application process.

25
00:01:09.524 --> 00:01:12.455
Review the actual HTTP-client and
deployment behavior.

26
00:01:12.535 --> 00:01:16.992
The component choosing the final socket
destination must enforce compatible policy,

27
00:01:17.072 --> 00:01:21.224
and proxy credentials must not become
ambient authority for arbitrary targets.

28
00:01:21.304 --> 00:01:25.762
A secure-looking application lookup is
insufficient if an intermediary later routes

29
00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:27.673
the request under different rules.

30
00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:31.783
Add network-level containment as
defense-in-depth: egress firewall rules,

31
00:01:31.863 --> 00:01:35.588
restricted subnets, proxy allowlists,
segmented service identities,

32
00:01:35.668 --> 00:01:38.293
and separation from sensitive internal
services.

33
00:01:38.373 --> 00:01:42.525
These controls limit what the workload can
reach when application validation is

34
00:01:42.605 --> 00:01:43.888
incomplete or bypassed.

35
00:01:43.968 --> 00:01:48.242
They do not know the caller's business
authorization and therefore cannot replace

36
00:01:48.322 --> 00:01:49.421
application policy.

37
00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:52.615
The strongest design combines a narrow
outbound operation,

38
00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:56.358
explicit destination authorization,
constrained request behavior,

39
00:01:56.438 --> 00:02:00.346
carefully scoped credentials, and a
network path that refuses unnecessary

40
00:02:00.426 --> 00:02:01.220
destinations.
